issue='The statement’s asymptotic target is recoverable, but a complete benchmark must fix the exact unit-cost real-arithmetic operations and the permitted randomness/error and worst-case versus expected running time. The source does not give a full machine definition. It should also confirm whether its use of ω=2 denotes the infimum-exponent hypothesis alone or an attained Õ(n²) matrix-multiplication algorithm; these hypotheses are not identical, so the latter is not substituted silently.'
save(i,dict(title='Constant-additive real-weighted APSP in near-quadratic time when ω=2',formal='Assuming matrix multiplication exponent ω=2, is there an algorithm and absolute constants C>0 and a≥0 that, on every n-vertex undirected graph with real edge weights in [0,1], outputs all-pairs distance estimates D̂ satisfying dist_G(u,v)≤D̂(u,v)≤dist_G(u,v)+C for every connected pair, in O(n²(log(n+1))^a) time? For disconnected pairs the output must be ∞. C is independent of n, the graph and the edge weights; the source asks whether even some large constant additive error is achievable, not necessarily every prescribed ε>0. The exact real-arithmetic and randomized-time conventions remain to be specified.',definitions='The input is a finite simple undirected graph G=(V,E), with V=[n] and an explicit real weight w(e)∈[0,1] for every edge. dist_G(u,v) is the minimum sum of edge weights on a path from u to v, equals zero when u=v, and is ∞ if no path exists. Zero-weight edges are allowed and negative weights are not. All n² estimates are output explicitly; a data structure answering only selected distance queries is a different output requirement. The inequalities forbid underestimation, and the tolerance C is additive in the normalized edge-weight scale rather than a factor times dist_G(u,v). For arbitrary bounded nonnegative weights the corresponding guarantee is C times the maximum edge weight after rescaling. Õ(n²) here denotes n² times a fixed polylogarithmic factor, not an unspecified n^{o(1)} factor. The matrix multiplication exponent is ordinarily the infimum of τ for which two s×s matrices can be multiplied using O(s^τ) arithmetic operations; ω=2 as an infimum supplies exponents arbitrarily close to two, but does not by itself assert an O(s² polylog s) algorithm. The paper measures arithmetic-algorithm running time on real-valued input, rather than Turing bit complexity for arbitrary finite-precision weights. Its exact primitive operations, uniformity and randomization conventions must be recorded before claiming a fully self-contained machine-level target.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='After fixing the source-compatible computational and matrix-exponent conventions, prove or refute existence of the stated algorithm for some absolute additive constant C. A positive answer must cover arbitrary real weights in [0,1] and all vertex pairs in the stated time. Unweighted-only results, multiplicative approximation, error growing with n, or a larger subpolynomial overhead do not by themselves meet the displayed target.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Small absolute error remains demanding on long paths',text='A constant multiplicative approximation may incur error proportional to the entire path length. The additive guarantee here permits only a constant number of maximum-edge-weight units of error, even when the shortest path uses many edges.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The source leaves a substantial gap at exponent two',text='The paper’s bound has exponent (3+ω²)/(ω+1), yielding 7/3 when ω is set to two, for any fixed positive additive tolerance. Its first final remark asks whether a near-quadratic bound could hold even if the allowed constant error is much larger.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Recent unweighted improvements do not settle real weights',text='The SODA 2026 work improves algorithms with additive errors two, four and six on unweighted undirected graphs. Its complete statements keep that unweighted assumption. These results therefore do not supply the arbitrary-real-weight guarantee required by the source question.',citation='unweighted2026'),dict(title='The matrix hypothesis and precision need care',text='An exponent defined as an infimum can differ from an attained bound with only logarithmic overhead. This distinction matters because the question explicitly requests Õ(n²). The card preserves that requested precision instead of silently replacing it by n^{2+o(1)}.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Remaining specification',text=issue)],why='Would determine whether a constant amount of additive error removes the gap between real-weighted all-pairs distances and the quadratic size of their output, even under the most optimistic matrix exponent.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2021',text='The source gives exponent (3+ω²)/(ω+1) for arbitrary fixed additive accuracy and poses the near-quadratic constant-error question.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2026-01-07',text='New additive-approximation bounds are published for the unweighted undirected case, without resolving the real-weighted target.',citation='unweighted2026')],status='source_open',status_note='Read the complete ESA 2021 paper, the full author preprint of the SODA 2026 unweighted follow-up, and current author/publication records. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no solution to the stated real-weighted target. Machine and exact exponent conventions remain unfinished.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',remaining_issue=issue,notes=['Restored the normalized real-weight model and existential constant-additive-error target.']),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Tests a substantial barrier between real-weighted shortest paths and near-output-linear computation under constant additive error. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Removed the section-heading debris, recovered the large-constant additive guarantee and defined distances, normalization and explicit all-pairs output. Separated later unweighted results and recorded the exact arithmetic/exponent conventions still needed.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The graph is undirected and its arbitrary real edge weights lie between zero and one.','The algorithm must output every distance with no underestimation and only a constant additive error.','The target time is n² up to polylogarithmic factors under the source’s ω=2 hypothesis.','The known source bound has exponent 7/3 when evaluated at ω=2, and recent unweighted improvements do not settle this real-weighted problem.','The precise real-arithmetic machine, randomization and exponent-attainment conventions remain to be fixed.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','unweighted2026','author'])),notes=['Recovered the full constant-additive approximation target and real-weight normalization.','Defined all-pairs output and the distinction from multiplicative or growing additive error.','Checked the 2026 unweighted follow-up and kept its scope separate.','Recorded the remaining arithmetic, randomness and exponent-precision choices.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.04775','https://tmc.web.engr.illinois.edu/apx_real_APSP_ESA21.pdf'],state='improved_unfinished')
